  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how the microenvironment supports malignant B cells through many receptor–ligand interactions at the cell surface. At the center is the lymphoma cell membrane, showing signaling receptors including the B‑cell receptor (BCR), PTCH/SMO for Hedgehog signaling, BCMA, TACI, BAFF‑R, interleukin receptors, CD40, and CD30. Inputs come from surrounding stromal cells and immune cells such as neutrophils, macrophages, T cells, eosinophils, and mast cells.\n\nThe diagram emphasizes that both antigen-driven and niche-derived signals promote survival and growth. Bacterial epitopes, autoantigens, and mannose-containing immunoglobulin appear to activate the BCR. Stromal cells provide lectin interactions and Hedgehog ligands, which feed into the PTCH/SMO pathway. Myeloid and stromal cells release APRIL and BAFF, which signal through BCMA, TACI, and BAFF‑R. Cytokines such as IL‑15, IL‑4, and IL‑13 act through IL receptors, while T cells and eosinophils engage CD40L and CD30L to stimulate CD40 and CD30.\n\nColored triangles indicate which lymphoma subtypes seem to use each pathway, including BL, DLBCL, MALT lymphoma, FL, Hodgkin’s lymphoma, and MCL. Overall, the image presents lymphoma as strongly dependent on external survival cues rather than isolated autonomous signaling.",
